# 4. TRANSFORM TRAINING MODE INTO SANDBOX MODE

Replace the existing Training game mode with a fully functional **SANDBOX MODE**.

The old Training mode should become an open environment where the player can freely spawn, test, fight, and experiment with every unit and enemy in the game.

This should not be a limited tutorial or a fixed training encounter.

It should be a proper sandbox where the player has control over what appears in the world.

### Sandbox spawning system

Allow the player to spawn every available unit and enemy, including:

- All playable mech units.
- All friendly AI units.
- All standard ground enemies.
- All flying enemies.
- All artillery units.
- All elite enemy variants.
- All boss variants.
- All REVENANT variants.
- All corrupted versions of playable units.
- Special event enemies.

The sandbox must automatically include newly added units and enemies as the game expands.

Do not require developers to manually rebuild the entire spawn menu every time a new unit is introduced.

Use a centralized unit and enemy registry that the sandbox can read.

### Spawn menu

Create a clean, minimalist military-themed spawn interface.

Organize spawnable entities into categories:

**PLAYER UNITS**
Every playable mech in the game.

**FRIENDLY UNITS**
Allied AI units and squadmates.

**HOSTILE UNITS**
Standard enemy mechs and ordinary enemies.

**ELITE UNITS**
Advanced enemy variants and specialized units.

**BOSSES**
Every boss and boss variant.

**REVENANTS**
Every REVENANT variant, including corrupted versions of all available player units.

Each entry should display:
- Unit name.
- Unit type.
- Small visual preview or icon.
- Team affiliation.
- Relevant variant information.

The interface should be easy to navigate without covering too much of the screen.

### Spawn controls

Allow players to:
- Select a unit.
- Spawn the selected unit.
- Spawn multiple copies.
- Choose whether it is friendly or hostile where supported.
- Set the number of units to spawn.
- Remove individual spawned units.
- Remove all spawned units.
- Respawn destroyed units.
- Reset the battlefield.

Add sensible spawn limits to prevent performance problems.

Allow the player to place spawned units in appropriate positions without creating immediate collisions or overlapping entities.

### AI behavior controls

Allow the player to configure spawned AI units.

Possible settings:
- Idle.
- Follow.
- Patrol.
- Aggressive.
- Defensive.
- Attack player.
- Attack hostile units.
- Friendly support.
- Boss behavior.

When appropriate, allow the player to switch team affiliation between friendly and hostile.

Make sure team changes correctly update targeting, damage, and AI relationships.

Do not allow units to attack themselves or their own team unless a specific game mechanic intentionally permits it.

### Sandbox environment controls

Include useful testing controls such as:
- Reset environment.
- Clear all spawned units.
- Pause and resume simulation.
- Reset unit health.
- Restore the player's health.
- Toggle invulnerability for testing.
- Toggle infinite ammunition for testing.
- Adjust enemy aggression.
- Adjust unit movement speed for testing, where technically safe.
- Spawn a REVENANT immediately.
- Spawn a boss immediately.

These controls should only affect Sandbox mode and should not modify normal progression or campaign balance.

### Testing different scenarios

The Sandbox should allow the player to create combat situations such as:

- One boss against the entire player squad.
- Several bosses fighting one another.
- A REVENANT fighting its original mech.
- Multiple REVENANT variants fighting together.
- Flying enemies attacking ground units.
- Heavy artillery units attacking fast-moving enemies.
- Four friendly units against a large enemy force.
- Multiple high-speed bosses running simultaneously.
- A large battle with several enemy types.

The player should be able to experiment without restarting the entire game after every test.

### REVENANT availability

All REVENANT variants must be available in the Sandbox regardless of whether the player has failed a game mode.

Sandbox spawning must bypass the normal failure-based REVENANT unlock or encounter trigger.

The player must be able to manually spawn any REVENANT at any time.

However, manually spawning a REVENANT in Sandbox must not modify the player's failed-run records or trigger a REVENANT encounter in another game mode.

### Sandbox progression isolation

Sandbox mode must not:
- Change campaign progression.
- Count as a failed game mode.
- Trigger the next-round REVENANT system.
- Award normal match progression.
- Alter saved unit upgrades.
- Modify normal game difficulty.
- Accidentally trigger victory or defeat sequences.

Keep Sandbox experimentation isolated from standard game progression.

If the game already uses a common save system, ensure Sandbox settings do not overwrite normal progression data.

